# HTTP client behaviour against the LithosAI endpoint

Transport cost and whether HTTP/2 pays, measured 2026-10-05 on this machine
against `https://api.lithosai.cloud/v1/models`. That endpoint answers an
unauthenticated `401` with a tiny body, so a reading here is the transport and
not the model; server-side prefill is in `research/lithos/latency.dj`, and the
vendor's own protocol support is in `research/lithos/lithos.dj`.

## Method

Every request is `--resolve`d to a real address (`3.220.29.237`), because this
machine's DNS maps every host into `198.18.0.0/15` — a transparent proxy whose
TCP connect returns in ~0.3 ms, far too fast to be a US round trip. The proxy
still sits in the path, so absolute times are not portable; the comparisons are.

Instruments: curl 8.22.0, `research/libcurl_probe.zig` for libcurl,
`research/zig_h1_bench.zig` for `std.http.Client`.

## A connection costs ~0.58 s, and only reuse removes it

| | TCP | TLS | TTFB |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| fresh connection | 0.3–0.5 ms | 0.51–0.60 s | 0.87–0.90 s |
| reused connection | — | — | 0.21–0.31 s |

`nconn=0` on the reused rows: no handshake. The TLS handshake is the whole
client-side cost, and reusing the connection reclaims ~0.58 s of it. One fresh
reading hit 1.59 s for TLS; the rest stayed in band.

Reuse is the endpoint's own `keep-alive`, so it is available to any client that
keeps the connection — `std.http.Client`'s pool included. HTTP/2 versus HTTP/1.1
does not enter into it for a single request: fresh, both are ~0.87–0.90 s.

## Concurrency: h2 multiplexes, h1.1 opens N connections

Wall time for N requests issued together, three trials each.

| N | client | connections | wall (s) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 10 | curl h1.1 | 10 | 0.76 / 0.83 / 2.14 |
| 10 | curl h2 | 1 | 0.92 / 0.81 / 0.90 |
| 10 | `std.http` h1 | 10 | 0.78 / 0.85 / 1.19 |
| 20 | curl h1.1 | 20 | 1.23 / 2.48 / 0.87 |
| 20 | curl h2 | 1 | 0.89 / 0.90 / 0.89 |
| 20 | `std.http` h1 | 20 | 0.89 / 1.71 / 1.09 |

- h2 carries all N streams on **one** connection; h1.1 needs one per in-flight
  request, so N TLS handshakes.
- At N=10 h1.1 and h2 are the same cost, because the N handshakes run in
  parallel and all finish about when one does. At N=20 h1.1 goes erratic
  (0.87–2.48 s) while h2 stays flat (0.89–0.90 s).
- curl h1.1 and `std.http.Client` h1 are the same transport, cost for cost.
- Measured on short requests. For long SSE streams h2 puts every stream on one
  TCP connection, so a stalled connection stalls all of them — the opposite of
  N independent h1.1 sockets. `[INFERENCE]` not measured here.

## "Force h2" is the client's job, not libcurl's

libcurl does not enforce the version. `research/libcurl_probe.zig` with
`CURL_HTTP_VERSION_2TLS` against `https://ftp.gnu.org/` (ALPN `http/1.1` only):

```
h2: status=403 negotiated=HTTP/1.1
abort: h2 was required and not negotiated
```

The request **succeeded over HTTP/1.1**; libcurl fell back without a word. So a
client that must run h2 has to read `CURLINFO_HTTP_VERSION` after the response
starts and abort on anything but `CURL_HTTP_VERSION_2_0`. Against the endpoint
that matters it does negotiate h2 — `api.lithosai.cloud`, `h2` required:

```
h2: status=401 negotiated=HTTP/2
```

## curl options that do not help

Against a fresh connection to the endpoint, none of these moved the handshake
out of its 0.57–0.60 s band, and none helped the reused path:

| option | effect |
| --- | --- |
| `--tcp-fastopen` | none |
| `--no-tcp-nodelay` | none |
| DNS caching | none — name lookup is ~0.02 ms through the proxy |
| picking another address | none — all three A records handshake in 0.57–0.60 s |
| `--http2` vs `--http1.1` on one request | none |

TLS session resumption was not established: `openssl -sess_out` wrote no session
file (TLS 1.3 tickets arrive after the handshake, and the test closed first), so
it is untested rather than ruled out.

The only lever is the connection itself.